│ Reasoning: connector_agent explicitly reports migration_feasible=False and compatible=False, with detailed reasoning that gmqtt's asyncio-native            │
│ connect/publish model and callback-based lifecycle cannot be reconciled with MqttSink's fully synchronous constructor-calls-connect()-then-publish() design │
│ without a structural rewrite. The generated 'wrapper' does not actually resolve this: it spins up a background thread running a persistent asyncio loop and │
│ blocks on run_coroutine_threadsafe(...).result() for every connect/publish call, which is exactly the kind of ad-hoc architectural bridge the connector     │
│ warned would be required, not a thin adapter. More importantly, the wrapper's own docstrings admit it cannot preserve paho's real semantics:                │
│ _AxiomPublishResult always returns rc=0 regardless of actual broker acknowledgment, silently discarding the real delivery-status contract that call sites   │
│ (getattr(info, 'rc', 0)) depend on for QoS>=1 correctness. Clean syntax/pylint/bandit/mypy and a passing import-only smoke test do not exercise actual      │
│ connect()/publish() against a broker or across threads/loops, so they don't demonstrate the semantic and lifecycle mismatches raised by connector_agent are │
│ actually resolved. This is a structural/semantic incompatibility in gmqtt's design relative to the call sites, not a fixable wrapper bug.                   │
